      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector",
      "problem": "Let $a=⟨-2,8,3⟩.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $a$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}\\cdot ⟨-2,8,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
